Target is the proposition that for all types Fm (of sentences), S₀, and S (of proof systems), equipped with classical propositional connectives (implication 🡒, conjunction ⋏, disjunction ⋎, negation ∼) and constants (truth ⊤, falsehood ⊥) on Fm and with provability relations (⊢) on Fm for S₀ and for S, for all proof systems 𝓑 in S₀ and 𝓢 in S such that both 𝓑 and 𝓢 prove all classical propositional tautologies and are closed under modus ponens, and for all arbitrary functions K, F : Fm → Fm from sentences to sentences, if the following six hypotheses hold:
1. (Def1 𝓑 K) For every sentence φ, if 𝓑 proves φ, then 𝓑 proves K φ;
2. (𝓑 ⪯ 𝓢) Every sentence provable in 𝓑 is provable in 𝓢;
3. (Def2 𝓢 K) For all sentences φ and ψ, 𝓢 proves (K (φ 🡒 ψ)) 🡒 ((K φ) 🡒 (K ψ));
4. (Def4 𝓢 K) For every sentence φ, 𝓢 proves K ((K φ) 🡒 φ);
5. 𝓑 proves (F (∼(F ⊥))) 🡒 (F ⊥);
6. For every sentence φ, 𝓢 proves K ((F φ) 🡒 (K φ));
then 𝓢 proves K (∼(K (∼(F ⊥)))).

Nothing else is required: in particular, neither 𝓑 nor 𝓢 is assumed to be consistent; Def3 (that 𝓢 proves (K φ) 🡒 φ for all φ) and Def5 (the existence of a sentence ψ such that 𝓑 proves ψ 🡘 ∼(F ψ)) are not assumed; rule Def1 is assumed only for 𝓑 and not for 𝓢; Def2 and Def4 are assumed only for 𝓢 and not for 𝓑; and K and F are arbitrary functions on Fm with no further syntactic, arithmetic, or deductive assumptions beyond those listed.

